WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.030
A practical private dashboard can run on a local server, workstation, or small cluster,

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:07.220
depending on scale and availability needs.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.170
Collectors or agents receive events from cameras, endpoints, network tools, sensors,

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:11.940
and applications.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.455
APIs support controlled integration when direct collection is not appropriate.

00:00:15.455 --> 00:00:19.565
A message queue can buffer events and separate producers from downstream processing.

00:00:19.565 --> 00:00:23.445
This helps absorb bursts and lets services recover without immediately losing data.

00:00:23.445 --> 00:00:26.205
A database or event store retains normalized records,

00:00:26.205 --> 00:00:30.430
while larger artifacts such as video clips may use separate storage paths with tighter

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.260
access and retention controls.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.720
Event normalization maps different source formats into a consistent structure.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.400
Common fields may include event time, source, category, severity, confidence, asset,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.303
location, status, and evidence link.

00:00:42.303 --> 00:00:46.545
Preserve the original record or a reference to it so normalization does not erase details

00:00:46.545 --> 00:00:48.080
needed for investigation.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.880
The dashboard frontend should query controlled services rather than connect directly to

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:53.200
every source.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.335
This reduces credential sprawl and creates one place to enforce authorization, pagination,

00:00:57.335 --> 00:00:59.150
filtering, and audit logging.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:03.000
Service accounts and API keys should have narrow privileges and protected storage.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.150
Design the flow from collection through review: source, collector, queue, normalizer,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.120
store, dashboard, notification, and analyst action.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.150
At each step, identify what happens during delay, duplication, malformed input,

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:16.585
storage failure, or loss of connectivity.

00:01:16.585 --> 00:01:19.760
Event identifiers and timestamps help prevent confusion.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:23.595
Privacy-aware architecture keeps sensitive content close to its legitimate purpose.

00:01:23.595 --> 00:01:28.170
Store clips separately from ordinary alert metadata, limit thumbnails where possible,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:32.730
minimize personal data, encrypt appropriate paths, and avoid public exposure.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:35.940
Architecture should make safe handling the default rather than relying on every user to

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:36.680
remember policy.
